Anti-convergence constraints (MUST follow):
1. Generic mood words may appear AT MOST ONCE per prompt and only if sampled: soft/warm lighting, glow, sparkle, petals, gentle breeze, golden hour, bloom, soft focus, bokeh and the like. Never stack them.
2. If the sampled camera tags contain no shot-size or angle tag, you may add at most one shot/angle word (`eye level`, `bird's-eye view`, `over-the-shoulder`, `extreme close-up`, `deep focus`, `close-up`, `upper body`, `full body`). Never add composition-rule words (`rule of thirds`, `leading lines`, `negative space`, `frame-in-frame`, `symmetrical composition`, `diagonal composition`, `midground`, `foreground focus`, `background focus`) — leave the composition to the visual style.
3. The expression slot must include at least one non-default emotion (surprised, confused, sad, angry, sleepy, serious, smug, scared, pensive, determined, flustered) unless the sampled expression tags only contain default ones.
4. The scene-description sentences must state what is concretely visible (objects, their arrangement, position or motion) NOT already in the tags. Do not add generic mood fillers (light, mist, wind, petals) that are not actual scene elements.

